Inverted index
The key data structure underlying modern IR (Information Retrieval)
For each term t, we must store a lst of all documents that contain t
    - identify each doc by a docID, a document serial number
Can we use fixed-sized array for this? - inefficient
    - we need variable posting lists
    terms in documents and posting list given a different document

    dictionary -> posting
    Brutus -> 1 2 4 11
    Caesar -> 1 2 4 5
    Calpurnia -> 2 31 54 101

    dictionary is relatively small, stored in memory
    posting - stored on disk

Text preprocessing
    - tokenization
        -- cut character sequence into word tokens
    - normalization
        -- map text and query term to the same form
    -- other mapping (stemming)
        -- dif forms of a root to match
    -- stop words
        -- we may omit very common words (or not)

Storage
    - number of terms relatively modest
    - pointer of where posting lists are
    - posting lists (large but manageable)

Questions
- how to do index efficiently
- how much storage we need

