Phrase queries
- match a pair of words as a unit, "stanford university"
- the sentence "I went to university at Stanford" is not a match
- many queries are implicit phrase queries, "san francisco"
- it no longer suffices to store only

1. biword indexes
       index every consecutive pair of terms in the text as a phrase
       for example, the text "friends, romans, countrymen" generates
            -- friends romans
            -- romans countrymen
       each of these biwords is now a dictionary term
       two-word phrase query-processing is now immediate
       longer phrases can be processed by breaking them down
        -- "stanform university palo alto" to be broken into
            boolean query on biwords
            "stanford university" AND "university palo" AND "palo alto"
            that is not perfectm can have false positive

    issues
        false positives
        index blowup due to bigger dictionary

2. Positional indexes (standard solution)
<term, number of doc containing term>

doc1 : position1, position2 ...
doc2 : position1...

Example
Which of docs 1,2,3 and 4 could contain
"to be or not to be"
<be > 993427> The word "be" occurs almost in mln of docs
1: 7, 18, 33, 72, 86, 231
2: 3, 149
4: 17, 191, 291, 430, 434
5: 363, 367 ...

it does not occur in document 3

- for phrase queries, we use a merge algorith recursively at the document level
- but we now need to deal with more than just equality

need an entry for each occurence, not just once per document

A positional index is 2-4 as large as a non-positional index
Positional indez size 35-50% of volume of original text

3. Combination schemes
- for particular phrases, "the who" - it is inefficient
- mixed indexing scheme
    common phrases - biwords
    less common - unique

    work out in advance the common biwords
    in reality, do it more dynamically

Ranked Retrieval
    - good boolean queries are not good for all the users
    - feast or famine problem : too few or too many
    - takes a lot of skills
    - AND : gives too few, OR too many results

Rather that a set of docs, return an ordering over the top
documents in the collection

Free text queries (associated with ranked retrieval models)
rathe than a query language of operations and expressions
the user query is one or more qords in a human language

Feast or famine does not exist in ranked retrieval

Good system scoring - how?
    - assign a score to each document
    how the query and document reflect each other

One term-query
    - if a query term does not occur, the score is zero
    - the more frequent the query term in the doc,
        the higher the score

Scoring with the Jaccard Coefficient
    - measure for the overlap btw set A and B
    - number of items in A and B and divide by
     jaccard(A, B) = |A intersection B | / |A union B|
     jaccard(A, A) = 1
     sets dont have to be same size, always btw 0 and 1
     Jaccard coef with be smaller for the smaller set, with same number of words

issues :
    - does not consider term frequency
    - rare terms are more informative than frequent terms
    - need a more sophisticated way of normalizing for length
    use cosine similarity score : |A intersect| / |sqrt (A union B)|

