Term frequency
    - number of times the term occurs
    - raw term frequency is not what we want
    - relevance goes up with the number of mentioning, but not linear

term frequency
    - rate
    - count in Information Retrieval

Log-frequency weighting
    1 + log10 (term frequency), if tf > 0
    0 otherwse (words does not occur)

to score a document-query pair
    - sum over terms t in both q and d
    score = SUM (1 + log (tf)t,d)
    calculate for each term and sum up
    score is 0 when none of query terms is present

Reverse document frequency
- Rare terms are more informative that frequent\ //
    -- stop words
- give a high weight to rare terms
- frequent terms
    -- positive weight by smaller
- use document frequency df

idf weight
- df_t if the document frequency of t
    the number of documents that contain t

    df_t is an inverse measure of the informativeness of t
    df_t <= N (vocabulary size)

Ex. collection is 1mln, the term occurs in 10 documents
    df_t = 10

idf_t = log 10 (N / df_t)

log is used to dampen, other wise too strong.
The base of log is not important, using 10 here.

The word that occurs in every document has the value zero.

There is one idf for each term, and it wll be

Does idf have effect on ranking for one-term queries
    - iPhone - No.
    log (N / df), scaling factor
    - only multiple terms in a query
    "capricious person"
        put more attention on first word

Collection frequency of t
number of occurrence in the collection

word      | collection frequency | document frequency
insurance | 10 440 | 3 997
try       | 10 422 | 8 760

when try occurs in the document it occurs once
insurance tends to occur two or three times

give higher weighting to words insurance appearing
"try to buy insurance"
    the most important word, desc
        - insurance
        - buy
        - try
and it is not captured by collection frequency

TF IDF Weighting, tf x idf

W = (1 + log tf) x log10 (N / df)

Where N is the size of full corpus, number of documents (titles)

increased wth the number of times the term occurs in document
goes up with the rarety of the term

score of query = SUM terms tf.idf

The Vector Space Model ----------
- We have a V dimensional vector space
- Terms are axes of the space
- Documents are points of vectors in this space
- Very high-dimensional
- They are sparse vectors

How do we handle queries?
- Represent vector as a vector in the space
- Rank documents acc to their proximity, similarity
more similar, higher
- distance btw points
    - Euclidean not optimal because large for different
    in terms of information retrieval model
    - use angle (cosine of angle) in vector space
        rank in decreasing angle
        rank in inc for cosine of angle

- Length of the vector
sqrt root of sum of sqrts
- Divide a vector by its L norm , normalize
- only do for the intersection for query and document

